  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: explain how relational database executes SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\n\nNeed produce final answer. Must be thorough. Need likely structure: overview pipeline: tokenizer/parser, binder/resolver, optimizer (logical/physical), executor, storage engine, concurrency control, network. Include examples SQL: orders, customers, products. Discuss parse, syntax tree, name resolution, permissions. Planner: logical plan, statistics, cardinality estimates, cost model, physical operators, join orders, index selection, access paths. Indexes: B-tree, hash, covering, partial, composites, selectivity, Sargability, indexes on foreign keys, covering indexes, index-only scans. Joins: nested loop, hash, merge; inner/outer; join order; semi/anti; tradeoffs. Transactions: ACID, isolation levels, locking, MVCC, snapshots, row-level vs table-level, write skew, deadlocks, serializable, idempotent updates, explicit BEGIN/COMMIT, read committed etc. Returning rows: cursor vs result set, fetch, serialization, projection, pagination, keyset vs OFFSET, streaming vs materialization, temp tables, spilling. Performance pitfalls: full scans, N+1 in app, non-sargable predicates, implicit conversions, functions in WHERE, leading wildcard, bad statistics, cardinality underestimation, join order, correlated subqueries, OR across tables, UNION vs UNION ALL, temp table vs CTE, index bloat, lock contention, small transactions, large transactions, OFFSET pagination, missing covering index, data skew, parallelism limits. Concrete examples with SQL and explain plan pseudo. Maybe include PostgreSQL EXPLAIN and indexes. Need avoid repeating. Complete sentences.\n\nNeed maybe mention different RDBMS variations: PostgreSQL/Oracle/MySQL. Use generic but concrete. Tutorial style.\n\nLet's craft with headings. Include example schema:\ncustomers(cust_id, name, region, email)\norders(order_id, cust_id, order_date, status, total)\norder_items(item_id, order_id, product_id, qty)\nproducts(product_id, name, category)\nIndexes: customers pk, orders(cust_id, order_date desc, status), order_items pk, order_items(order_id), products pk, products(category, name).\nQuery:\nSELECT c.name,",
